// ScaleRatio — the growth reading, and the ONLY one this instrument makes.
//
// THE LADDER DOUBLES. Every rung is twice the program of the rung below it, so the ratio between two
// consecutive rungs IS the growth, read straight off the raw numbers:
//
//     x2.00   linear        — twice the program, twice the allocations (or bytes, or CPU ticks)
//     x4.00   quadratic     — twice the program, four times as many
//     x1.00   constant      — the phase does not grow with the program at all
//
// There is nothing to fit, and that is the point. This file replaces a least-squares power-law fit
// over log-log points, which produced an EXPONENT — and an exponent is interpretation dressed up as
// measurement. It could not tell you anything the doubling ladder had not already told you, and it
// brought a train of machinery behind it that could: a residual, to say how well one power law
// described the curve; a NOISY verdict inferred from that residual; an exemption for the curves whose
// residual was large because they are a SUM of power laws. The end of that road was an agent editing
// the instrument so it would stop complaining. A ratio has no residual, no verdict and nothing to
// exempt, because a ratio is a division.
//
// IT IS THE SAME DIVISION IN EVERY COLUMN — BUT THE COLUMNS ARE NOT EQUALLY EXACT, AND A RATIO
// INHERITS WHATEVER ITS INPUTS CARRY. Allocation counts and byte volumes are bit-for-bit
// reproducible — the same compiler on the same corpus produces the same digits on an idle machine
// and a loaded one — so a ratio of them is exact, and a change in it is REAL. CPU ticks are not.
// They count only the compiling THREAD, which is what makes them trendable at all where wall time is
// not, but they still move a few percent with turbo, thermal throttling and cache pressure from
// other cores. So a ratio taken of CPU ticks carries that band and a ratio taken of allocations does
// not, and the two must not be read with the same confidence.
//
// THE BAND IS SMALL AGAINST THE QUESTION THE LADDER ASKS, WHICH IS WHY THE COLUMN IS WORTH HAVING.
// x2.00 against x4.00 is a 100% margin; a few percent cannot move a reading across it. And the CPU
// ratio answers the one question the exact columns structurally cannot — a pass that re-walks tokens
// and allocates almost nothing reads x1.00 in allocations however quadratic it is. What a CPU ratio
// may NOT be used for is a claimed constant-factor win of a few percent: that is inside the band, and
// the exact columns are where it belongs. The unit is platform-defined (TSC ticks on Windows,
// nanoseconds on POSIX) with no honest conversion between the two, which costs the RATIO nothing —
// a division cancels the unit — and is exactly why the ratio, not the absolute, is what travels.
//
// WALL TIME IS STILL NOT COLLECTED AT ALL, and that has not changed. It counts every other process
// on the box, so a dated table of it would compare a loaded machine in July against an idle one in
// August and conclude nothing. The metrics TSV carries a nanosecond column and the runner reads past
// it. (The COMPILER still times itself: `--metrics=<path>` and `--log=compiler:debug` report
// per-phase milliseconds for one compile, which is exactly where a wall-clock reading is worth having
// and where it is not being trended against a different machine on a different day.)

// A growth ratio in HUNDREDTHS: 200 is x2.00. Integer arithmetic end to end — the inputs are exact
// counters and there is no reason to launder them through a float on the way to a division.
export typealias GrowthRatio = int(0 to u64.max)

let RatioScale = 100 as GrowthRatio
let RatioFractionDigits = 2

// What a ratio is taken OF: an allocation count, a byte volume, a code size — and, since the CPU
// column landed, a CPU tick reading. One type for all four, because the division is the same division
// and a second copy of it differing only in the name of its parameter is exactly the duplication that
// has no reason to exist.
//
// ⚠ THE NAME IS NARROWER THAN WHAT IT HOLDS — a tick reading is not a memory count — and it is said
// here so a reader is not misled by it. What the type actually means is "a quantity a growth ratio may
// be taken of", which is the set `ScaleMeasure` (ScaleTestRunner) enumerates.
export typealias MeasuredQuantity = int(0 to u64.max)

// The growth of one rung over the rung below it.
//
// A union, not a ratio of zero, and not a "0" that a reader could mistake for "it shrank to nothing".
// A phase that allocated NOTHING at the lower rung has no baseline to have grown from — that is a
// different statement from any number, and it is said differently.
export union GrowthReading
	measured(ratio GrowthRatio)
	noBaseline
end 'GrowthReading'

export function growthOf(now MeasuredQuantity, previous MeasuredQuantity) returns GrowthReading
	if previous == 0 'nothingToGrowFrom'
		return GrowthReading.noBaseline
	end 'nothingToGrowFrom'

	// `previous` is guarded non-zero above; `otherwise panic` is the project's form for a `try` that must not fail.
	return GrowthReading.measured((try ((now * RatioScale) / previous) otherwise panic("growthOf: previous was 0 past its own guard")) as GrowthRatio)
end 'growthOf'

// `x2.00`, or `-` when there is no rung below to compare against (rung 0), or when the rung below
// allocated nothing.
export function growthText(reading GrowthReading) returns String
	return match reading 'r'
		noBaseline gives NoBaselineText
		measured(ratio) gives "x{ratio / RatioScale}.{zeroPad2(ratio mod RatioScale)}"
	end 'r'
end 'growthText'

// It is `-` and not `0.00`, for the reason the union exists: an absent reading must not look like a
// value. Nothing parses this — the trend log records absolute counts, never ratios — so it is chosen
// to be read by a human and by nobody else.
export let NoBaselineText = "-"

// Two digits always: `x2.5` would read as two and a half when the value is two-point-oh-five.
function zeroPad2(fraction GrowthRatio) returns String
	if fraction < 10 'oneDigit'
		return "0{fraction}"
	end 'oneDigit'

	return "{fraction}"
end 'zeroPad2'

// Is `now` at least `minimum` times `previous`? The comparison is done by cross-multiplying rather
// than by taking the ratio and comparing that, so nothing is lost to integer truncation at the one
// place in this suite where a comparison decides whether the run is BROKEN (ScaleCorpus's degeneracy
// check).
export function grewBy(now MeasuredQuantity, previous MeasuredQuantity, minimum GrowthRatio) returns bool
	return now * RatioScale >= previous * (minimum as MeasuredQuantity)
end 'grewBy'
